      <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-2 mb-4">Markdown in SQLite: 40× Fewer Tokens for AI Agents (2026)</h1>

      <p class="mb-4">On October 29, 2026, the research lab Native published a research preview that reframes a conversation the Markdown tooling community has been having for two years. The question: as AI agents take over more of the reading, retrieval, and synthesis work we used to do ourselves, is plain Markdown still the right container for our knowledge? Native's answer is nuanced — Markdown stays, but it needs a query layer. Their benchmark shows that wrapping a Markdown vault in SQLite and giving agents access via an MCP server cuts token consumption by 40×, reduces failed trials by 97%, and delivers answers 6× faster than the grep-based Markdown approach.</p>

      <p class="mb-4">The finding matters for anyone who writes in Markdown and increasingly delegates the surrounding work — research, drafting, cross-referencing — to AI agents. It also clarifies something important about the Markdown pipeline: the format is not the bottleneck. The retrieval pattern is.</p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">What the benchmark actually measured</h2>

      <p class="mb-4">Native's test setup was deliberately adversarial to naive Markdown retrieval. They built a vault of approximately 11,000 Markdown files — representing roughly two months of accumulated context — and asked a set of questions that required agents to find, cross-reference, and synthesize information across multiple files. Three retrieval architectures were compared:</p>

      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-4">
        <li><strong>Markdown + wikilinks</strong> &mdash; the standard Obsidian-style vault, where agents use grep and follow wikilink trails to find relevant content. This is the baseline most knowledge workers use today.</li>
        <li><strong>Native-to-Markdown</strong> &mdash; Native's own export layer, which pre-processes and structures the vault content for agent consumption.</li>
        <li><strong>Native's SQLite MCP</strong> &mdash; the Markdown content indexed into a SQLite database, exposed to agents through the Model Context Protocol, so agents query structured tables instead of searching raw text.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="mb-4">The results, summarized in Native's published chart data:</p>

