      <p class="mb-4">The latency result is counterintuitive: the SQLite approach was not slower despite adding a database layer. Median time to a fully correct answer dropped to one-sixth of the grep-based baseline. Native attributes this to agents spending far fewer tokens on irrelevant context — the database query returns only what is needed, rather than entire files that must then be filtered in-context.</p>

      <p class="mb-4">It is worth noting Native's own caveat: these results apply to the tested workload — a large, link-heavy vault at the 11,000-file scale. They do not establish a universal advantage for every vault size or query type. For a vault of a few hundred files, the overhead of maintaining a SQLite index likely outweighs the token savings.</p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">Why Markdown itself is not the problem</h2>

      <p class="mb-4">A superficial reading of this research might suggest that Markdown is being displaced — that agents need structured databases, not plain text files. That is the wrong conclusion. Native's architecture keeps the Markdown files as the canonical source. The SQLite database is a derived index, built from the Markdown, that agents query instead of reading raw files. When a human needs to edit, review, or port the content, the Markdown files are still there — readable, diffable, and portable.</p>

      <p class="mb-4">This is the right separation of concerns. Markdown excels at human authorship: it is plain text, it diffs cleanly in version control, it renders everywhere, and it will be readable in fifty years without special software. What Markdown is not good at — at scale — is selective retrieval. A grep search across 11,000 files returns too much noise. A wikilink trail requires the agent to already know which links matter. A SQL query against a well-indexed table returns exactly what is asked for.</p>

      <p class="mb-4">The pattern is the same one that has played out in publishing repeatedly: the human-authored source stays in the most portable, durable format available, and machine-optimized indexes are built on top of it. HTML documents with search-engine indexes. Source code with compiled artifacts. Markdown with SQLite.</p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">What this means for the Markdown publishing pipeline</h2>

      <p class="mb-4">For people whose primary use of Markdown is writing and publishing — not building agent infrastructure — the immediate implication is validation, not disruption. Markdown is increasingly the format agents read and write. That means the drafts coming out of your AI-assisted workflow are already in the right format for every stage of the pipeline: agent retrieval, human review, and platform publishing.</p>

      <p class="mb-4">The stage that is still underserved is the last one. LinkedIn, X Articles, Medium, and Notion all accept rich paste — bold, headers, bullet lists, links, code blocks — but none of them parse Markdown syntax from a plain-text paste. Getting a Markdown draft from your vault into a LinkedIn post still requires a conversion step. That step is where a purpose-built tool earns its place.</p>

