than grep-based Markdown search across an 11,000-file vault.

- Failed trials dropped **97%** at a 1M input budget
- Median answer time: **1/6** of the Markdown baseline
- Markdown files remain the human-readable source of truth</code></pre>

      <p class="mb-4">Pasting that directly into LinkedIn shows the raw syntax — asterisks, hyphens, hash marks. The platform does not render it. Running the same Markdown through <a href="https://md2rich.com/" class="text-indigo-600 font-semibold">md2rich</a> converts it to rich text: real bold, a proper bullet list, a formatted heading. Paste the output into LinkedIn and it renders exactly as written. No upload, no account, no server round-trip — the conversion happens in your browser.</p>

      <p class="mb-4">The privacy alignment between the two stages is worth making explicit. Native's architecture is local-first: the Markdown files and SQLite index live on your machine or in a self-hosted cloud, not in a SaaS platform's proprietary store. md2rich extends the same principle to the publishing stage. Nothing you paste into md2rich leaves your browser. For pre-embargo content, confidential drafts, or anything you would rather not route through a third-party server, the entire pipeline — from agent retrieval through Markdown editing to rich-text publishing — can stay on your machine.</p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">The practical workflow in 2026</h2>

      <p class="mb-4">Putting the pieces together, the Markdown-centric content workflow for a writer or small team looks like this:</p>

      <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 mb-4">
        <li><strong>Author in Markdown</strong> — write directly, or use an agent to draft and research against your vault. Keep the files in plain text with wikilinks where useful.</li>
        <li><strong>Add a query layer if scale demands it</strong> — if you are running agents against thousands of files, evaluate SQLite indexing (Native is open-sourcing their engine). For hundreds of files, grep is still fine.</li>
        <li><strong>Review and finalize the Markdown</strong> — human judgment on tone, accuracy, and structure before anything publishes.</li>
